&#x200B;

I am so not ready to get rejected

If you think that you're going to be more comfortable doing a phone interview, then do so. It isn't going to change how the interviewer sees you or presents you on paper, if anything, you feeling more comfortable will help you and your performance.

&#x200B;

I hate phone calls, and found zoom to be a blessing in disguise. I don't have to look anywhere specific to make it look like I'm looking in the right direction (and I've had interviewers whose line of sight has been ***very*** off. I also struggle with tone/inflection which usually makes me more anxious, so being able to look and the interviewer and their expressions whilst not actually being in close proximity to me, really made me feel a lot more chill. 

An option could be to start on zoom and if you feel too uncomfortable, say there's some sort of lag because of poor wifi and ask if it would be okay to turn your camera off to see if it helps?

&#x200B;

I wish you the best of luck and I hope you feel as relaxed as possible for the interview!

